    "reading": {
        "body": "Boston Dynamics holds 249 granted US patents, and its recent grants are about robot perception, docking, and offline behavior optimization rather than new mechanical forms.\n\nThe patent record is the only substantive signal here. Five grants from August and September 2026 cover dropped object detection, offline optimization to robot behavior, robust docking under imperfect sensing, perception for a mobile manipulator, and perception and fitting for a stair tracker. That cluster points to the software layer of legged and mobile robots: sensing, control policy, and the ability to operate when inputs are noisy. It is the work that turns a machine that can walk into one that can work in an unstructured building.\n\nNothing in the data ties this company to AI compute, data centers, or power. There are no products, no cases, and no MCP servers listed, and the company's own website returned only a placeholder, so there is no self-description to weigh against the patents. The 191 Massachusetts legal exposure entries are a count without detail, and the data does not say what they concern.\n\nWhat the record does not show is equally important. It gives no revenue, no customers, no deployment sites, and no indication of how these patents relate to any AI model or training run. A reader should treat the patent count as evidence of sustained engineering in embodied robotics, not as evidence of AI scaling demand.",
